features_verified:
IMPLEMENTED (read in regexp.c and probed with engine_cli): byte literals (runs collapsed into one EXACTLY node); '.' = any byte except NUL (so it matches 
); '^' = start of string only (BOL compares against regbol; no multiline), '$' = end of string only (NUL; NOT before a trailing 
 as in Python/Perl); '[...]' with byte-value ranges a-z, '[^...]' negation, ']' literal when first, '-' literal when first/last, NO escapes inside brackets (backslash is an ordinary member: '[\]]' = class {\} followed by literal ']', '[a\-z]' = a plus range \-z); quantifiers * + ? greedy with full backtracking (single-char operands use STAR/PLUS nodes + regrepeat, others use BRANCH/BACK loops); alternation '|' with leftmost-first (Perl-style) branch preference - confirmed by the man page AMBIGUITY section ('(ab|a)b*c' picks 'ab'); capturing groups '(...)' up to 9 (NSUBEXP=10; 10th -> 'too many ()'), reporting the LAST iteration like Perl (verified: (a|b)+c on xxabac -> group1 [4,5]); empty branches and empty groups allowed ('a|', '()' , '|'); '\c' = literal c for EVERY c (so \d = 'd', 
 = 'n', \. = '.'); regcomp errors (returned as NULL after calling regerror): unmatched/unterminated (), unmatched [], invalid [] range (start>end), '?+* follows nothing', 'nested *?+' (stacked quantifiers a** and also a*?), '*+ operand could be empty' ((a*)*, (|a)+, ()+ are REJECTED), too many (), trailing backslash, regexp too big. Search-anywhere is built in (regexec scans start positions, with regstart/strchr and regmust/strstr prefilters). NOT IMPLEMENTED: {n,m} {n} {n,} ('{' is a literal); lazy *? +? ?? (rejected as nested quantifier); (?:...) and any (?...) syntax (rejected); \d \w \s \D \W \S;   \B; 
 	 \xHH escapes; case-insensitive mode (no flag, no API); UTF-8 / code-point awareness (pure byte matcher; '.' and [] consume one byte, quantifier after a multibyte literal binds to its last byte); multiline anchors; '.' excluding newline; quantifier on possibly-empty subexpression; more than 9 groups; text with embedded NUL / length-delimited input.
